1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:01,757
Right now, Earth has two moons.

2
00:00:02,250 --> 00:00:07,386
The second is a tiny rock called 2026 QQ8, just a meter or two across.

3
00:00:07,386 --> 00:00:09,954
Earth's gravity caught it in April.

4
00:00:10,450 --> 00:00:11,537
It loops around us,

5
00:00:11,537 --> 00:00:15,257
never closer than about one and a half times the Moon's distance.

6
00:00:15,257 --> 00:00:17,088
It should leave early next year.

7
00:00:17,583 --> 00:00:21,089
But what if the second moon were a twin of ours, at half the distance?

8
00:00:21,389 --> 00:00:23,339
Like and subscribe for more what-ifs.

9
00:00:23,850 --> 00:00:26,962
It would look twice as wide, and four times as bright.

10
00:00:26,962 --> 00:00:28,922
Everything would cast two shadows.

11
00:00:29,417 --> 00:00:32,335
Its pull on the oceans would be eight times the Moon's.

12
00:00:32,850 --> 00:00:36,067
And sooner or later, the two moons would probably collide.

13
00:00:36,583 --> 00:00:37,984
Full story on the channel.
